WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.769
Vision Transformers classify images remarkably well, yet how they pull classes apart inside is poorly understood.

2
00:00:07.465 --> 00:00:13.886
This video follows class separation through a Vision Transformer, layer by layer and epoch by epoch.

3
00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:25.553
A Vision Transformer cuts an image into patches, projects each patch to a vector, and passes the sequence through a stack of transformer blocks, followed by a classifier head.

4
00:00:25.143 --> 00:00:32.155
The question is how the classes separate as the data moves through those blocks, and how that changes while the model trains.

5
00:00:31.837 --> 00:00:40.614
To find out, the representations are recorded after every block and throughout training, and a single number scores how well the classes are separated.

6
00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.711
For other deep networks, there is already an answer.

7
00:00:44.408 --> 00:00:52.128
Feedforward, convolutional, residual and densely connected networks follow a law of equi-separation.

8
00:00:51.667 --> 00:00:54.987
They separate data at a constant geometric rate through their depth:

9
00:00:55.684 --> 00:01:01.094
the logarithm of the separation measure falls in a straight line, layer after layer.

10
00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:06.023
That study left out transformers, whose self-attention mixes information across the whole image.

11
00:01:06.626 --> 00:01:09.935
Does the same law hold for a Vision Transformer?

12
00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:13.988
Take n samples from K classes, as represented at layer l.

13
00:01:14.801 --> 00:01:19.584
Each class has its own mean, and all the samples together have a global mean.

14
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:24.448
The within-class scatter adds up how far each sample lies from the mean of its class.

15
00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:31.324
The between-class scatter adds up how far each class mean lies from the global mean, weighted by the size of the class.

16
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:40.005
Separation fuzziness, D, is the trace of the within-class scatter times the pseudo-inverse of the between-class scatter.

17
00:01:40.701 --> 00:01:44.753
The pseudo-inverse guards against a singular between-class scatter.

18
00:01:44.433 --> 00:01:50.795
In words, it measures how spread out each class is, relative to how far apart the classes are.

19
00:01:50.287 --> 00:01:53.387
As the clusters tighten and drift apart, D falls.

20
00:01:54.083 --> 00:01:57.625
A lower D means better separated classes.

21
00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:02.678
The study trains the standard Vision Transformer on four datasets of growing complexity:

22
00:02:03.375 --> 00:02:09.331
MNIST, Fashion-MNIST, CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100.

23
00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:16.299
The default model has seven layers, an embedding dimension of sixty-four, eight attention heads and seven-by-seven patches.

24
00:02:17.112 --> 00:02:21.767
It trains on Fashion-MNIST for one hundred fifty epochs with Adam.

25
00:02:21.383 --> 00:02:23.659
Then one setting at a time is changed:

26
00:02:24.262 --> 00:02:34.433
depth, embedding dimension, attention heads, MLP dimension, patch size, dropout, optimizer and learning-rate schedule.

27
00:02:34.117 --> 00:02:39.365
Every setting is run with ten different seeds, and D is computed at every layer.

28
00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.901
Here is the default model at the end of training.

29
00:02:42.504 --> 00:02:48.344
The raw Fashion-MNIST images have a separation fuzziness of about twenty-three.

30
00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:50.994
After the first transformer block it jumps to about ninety-seven.

31
00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:55.533
The first block leaves the classes less separated, not more.

32
00:02:54.950 --> 00:03:01.173
From there it decays exponentially, block by block, to about two point seven at the last layer.

33
00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:09.279
The same spike, then decay, appears on MNIST, CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100, at very different scales.

34
00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.591
The paper calls this the Spike-Decay law.

35
00:03:12.288 --> 00:03:20.716
From the first layer on, D of l is D of one times e to the minus gamma l, with a decay constant gamma.

36
00:03:20.333 --> 00:03:24.257
So a Vision Transformer does not separate at a steady rate from the start.

37
00:03:24.861 --> 00:03:27.810
It first spikes, then decays.

38
00:03:28.073 --> 00:03:30.291
The pattern survives changes in depth.

39
00:03:30.894 --> 00:03:37.547
With five, seven, ten, twelve or fifteen layers, fuzziness spikes at the first block and then decays.

40
00:03:37.983 --> 00:03:41.861
Deeper models level off, so the extra layers add little separation.

41
00:03:42.557 --> 00:03:44.276
Training accuracy agrees:

42
00:03:44.717 --> 00:03:48.571
it stays around ninety-eight point five percent at every depth.

43
00:03:48.217 --> 00:03:56.147
The same shape holds across MLP dimension, attention heads, dropout, optimizer, learning-rate schedule and patch size.

44
00:03:56.843 --> 00:04:01.777
Scaled by their first-layer value, all these curves nearly coincide.

45
00:04:01.413 --> 00:04:08.611
Across these settings, the exponential fit over the layers never scores an R-squared below zero point nine seven.

46
00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:10.860
The pattern is there from the very start.

47
00:04:11.371 --> 00:04:18.662
After a single epoch, fuzziness already spikes at the first block, at about three hundred twenty, and decays after it.

48
00:04:18.347 --> 00:04:22.410
As training goes on, every layer's fuzziness falls, fast at first.

49
00:04:23.014 --> 00:04:26.195
In the last epochs the values barely change.

50
00:04:26.483 --> 00:04:28.898
Now follow only the last layer through training.

51
00:04:29.711 --> 00:04:38.047
Its fuzziness starts at about six point six, drops to about three point four within ten epochs, and settles near two point seven.

52
00:04:38.423 --> 00:04:39.630
This is the second law:

53
00:04:40.327 --> 00:04:44.170
the last layer's fuzziness decays exponentially over the epochs.

54
00:04:44.983 --> 00:04:50.312
D of t is D of zero times e to the minus lambda t.

55
00:04:49.967 --> 00:04:54.425
It holds provided the first law holds, and it shows no spike.

56
00:04:53.757 --> 00:04:57.008
The same decay appears on all four datasets.

57
00:04:57.227 --> 00:05:07.490
The second law, too, holds across depth, MLP dimension, attention heads, dropout, optimizer, learning-rate schedule and patch size.

58
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:10.135
Statistical tests over the seeded runs back both laws.

59
00:05:10.831 --> 00:05:19.771
For the first, no configuration shows a non-positive jump from the raw data to the first layer, or a poor exponential fit across the layers.

60
00:05:19.367 --> 00:05:20.783
The second law is weaker.

61
00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:32.184
Its exponential fits average an R-squared close to zero point nine eight, but small bumps break strict monotonicity, so a Mann-Kendall trend test often fails.

62
00:05:31.817 --> 00:05:38.086
So both laws hold, the second sometimes loosely, as long as the model learns from the data.

63
00:05:38.367 --> 00:05:43.615
The settings change how fast separation tightens, not whether it tightens at all.

64
00:05:43.233 --> 00:05:51.534
Fitted per epoch, the decay rate is about one point five six with seven-by-seven patches, and zero point five six with whole-image patches.

65
00:05:52.231 --> 00:05:57.258
The half-life grows from zero point four four to one point two four epochs.

66
00:05:56.897 --> 00:06:04.699
A larger embedding slows it too, from one point two two at thirty-two dimensions to zero point three nine at two hundred fifty-six.

67
00:06:05.396 --> 00:06:11.247
Cosine and step schedules sit at zero point four eight and zero point seven five.

68
00:06:11.683 --> 00:06:16.501
CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 decay fastest, at about two point nine.

69
00:06:17.105 --> 00:06:25.569
Plain SGD and ASGD almost double Adam's rate, and decrease monotonically in ten runs out of ten.

70
00:06:25.723 --> 00:06:28.451
The paper also sets fuzziness against attention.

71
00:06:29.148 --> 00:06:37.240
Ten models of the default setting were measured, layer by layer, for their fuzziness and for the entropy of their attention weights.

72
00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:42.552
Fuzziness peaks right after the patch embedding, exactly where attention entropy is lowest.

73
00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:46.161
Over the next layers, fuzziness falls while attention entropy rises.

74
00:06:46.857 --> 00:06:50.781
The classes sharpen as the attention changes its structure.

75
00:06:51.003 --> 00:06:52.141
There is one exception.

76
00:06:52.837 --> 00:06:58.480
With embedding dimensions of two hundred fifty-six and five hundred twelve, both laws break.

77
00:06:58.483 --> 00:07:02.581
At two hundred fifty-six, fuzziness jumps up and down from layer to layer.

78
00:07:03.278 --> 00:07:09.118
At five hundred twelve it grows through the layers, to nearly seven hundred thousand at the output.

79
00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:11.842
Over the epochs, the last layer's fuzziness no longer decays.

80
00:07:12.353 --> 00:07:13.873
It rises.

81
00:07:13.573 --> 00:07:14.966
Accuracy falls with it:

82
00:07:15.663 --> 00:07:22.536
to about seventy-four percent at two hundred fifty-six, and to about ten percent, chance level, at five hundred twelve.

83
00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:24.129
Pictures of the representations agree.

84
00:07:24.825 --> 00:07:29.667
The clusters overlap at two hundred fifty-six and fall apart at five hundred twelve.

85
00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:33.011
Diagnostics over ten seeded runs show why.

86
00:07:33.615 --> 00:07:41.069
From thirty-two to two hundred fifty-six dimensions, the condition number of the within-class scatter grows by six orders of magnitude:

87
00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:44.854
its variance squeezes into a thin subspace.

88
00:07:44.657 --> 00:07:50.079
The effective dimensionality falls from about twelve to one, although the embedding is sixteen times larger.

89
00:07:50.775 --> 00:07:53.991
The within-class scatter collapses to rank one.

90
00:07:53.657 --> 00:07:58.057
Fuzziness itself drops by four orders of magnitude at five hundred twelve.

91
00:07:58.754 --> 00:08:00.472
That is not better separation:

92
00:08:01.169 --> 00:08:07.078
the between-class scatter is nearly singular, which breaks the full-rank assumption behind the laws.

93
00:08:06.703 --> 00:08:15.434
A linear probe on the frozen features falls to fifteen percent accuracy, and the silhouette score turns negative from two hundred fifty-six on.

94
00:08:15.723 --> 00:08:29.052
In short, a Vision Transformer separates classes in a Spike-Decay pattern across its layers, and its last layer's fuzziness decays exponentially over training, unless the embedding is too large for the model to learn.

95
00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:33.947
Most of the separation happens in the early layers, so depth should be just enough.

96
00:08:34.457 --> 00:08:39.949
And fuzziness could serve as a training monitor, or a signal to stop once it levels off.

97
00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:47.263
Open questions include other transformer variants, other loss functions, language models, and a theory of why these laws appear.
